WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.630
Is the early buzz for "Oppenheimer" a sign
of a serious breakthrough, or is it a dud?

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.349
In their early reviews, critics are basking
in the glow of what might just be a masterpiece.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:14.330
J. Robert Oppenheimer changed the world as
we know it when he led The Manhattan Project

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.869
to develop the atomic bomb, and his story
is on its way to the silver screen.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.950
Since its announcement, the biopic "Oppenheimer"
has been the talk of the cinematic world,

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.600
and for good reason.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.030
With Christopher Nolan in the director's chair,
Ludwig Göransson responsible for the musical

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.620
score, and a stellar cast — led by "Peaky
Blinders" favorite and frequent Nolan collaborator

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.714
Cillian Murphy — the talent involved alone
seemed to set up the historical drama for success.

00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:42.940
There's also a considerable amount of pre-release
hype for "Oppenheimer," thanks in part to

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.170
its shared premiere date with director Greta
Gerwig's "Barbie."

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.340
The excitement for double features of the
two seeming polar-opposite films has been

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.450
palpable, to say the least.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:55.920
It's a perhaps improbable reason for such
positive word of mouth, but it's working.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.300
"More than 20,000 people have already bought
tickets to see both movies on the same day."

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.600
There's also the matter of what critics who
saw it early are saying.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.080
"Oppenheimer" held its world premiere in Paris
on July 11, and soon after, early thoughts

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.080
flooded Twitter.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.400
Based on those early reviews, it looks like
Nolan has hit yet another cinematic home run.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.600
After describing "Oppenheimer" as "fearsome,"
Vulture's Bilge Ebiri called it,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.680
"A relentlessly paced, insanely detailed,
intricate historical drama that builds and

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:30.280
builds and builds until Nolan brings the hammer
down in the most astonishing, shattering way."

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.460
The Sunday Times' Jonathan Dean also gave
"Oppenheimer" a glowing review, specifically

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.110
touting the film's tension and the acting
work of Murphy, Matt Damon, Robert Downey

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.430
Jr., and Alden Ehrenreich.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.450
While also giving Murphy high marks, TotalFilm's
Matt Maytum added the scale, the sound, and

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.469
visuals were awe-inspiring.

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:52.430
Given the subject matter of "Oppenheimer,"
the development of the atomic bomb, it shouldn't

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.145
be too surprising that it's also a harrowing
watch.

00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:56.970
"This is a matter of life and death."

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.000
GQ France's Adam Sanchez commented,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.070
"Christopher Nolan wanders in the mind of
#Oppenheimer and transforms the ravages of

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.619
the past into worries for the world of tomorrow."

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:09.050
Sanchez also described the film as "an extraordinary
experience."

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.993
Likewise, The Telegraph's Robbie Collin said
he was moved to tears by the time the credits rolled.

00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:18.830
Elsa Keslassy of Variety reported that audiences
were so impacted by the film that they stayed

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.360
at the Grand Rex Theater after it ended to
discuss and debate it.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.830
Nolan has a pretty stellar track record as
a director.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.750
An "Interstellar" one, even.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:32.010
The "Dark Knight" trilogy, "Inception," "Memento,"
and even sometimes-overlooked efforts like

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.110
"The Prestige" all left moviegoers floored
in their time, amounting to one of the most

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.720
impressive filmographies of the modern era.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.690
Despite how remarkable those efforts were,
some folks aren't afraid to call it early:

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:47.340
Of the films he's made over his career, "Oppenheimer"
is one of, if not, the absolute best Nolan

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.530
has ever directed.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:50.989
Writer and producer Ben Mekler tweeted,

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:53.569
"#OPPENHEIMER may be Nolan's masterpiece."

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:57.120
He predicted that it will more than likely
be an Oscar contender down the road.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.239
Jonathan Dean put "Oppenheimer" alongside
"Memento" and "The Prestige" in his personal

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:03.069
top three Nolan movies.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:07.450
Simon Thompson of The Playlist wasn't afraid
to apply words like "perfect" and "impeccable"

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.060
to the feature and some of its key elements.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.470
The countdown to the theatrical release of
"Oppenheimer" hits zero on July 21.

